\chapter{Menus}\label{ch:commands}

Everything the user can do to an image goes through the menu bar: derive a copy with a filter or
a resampler, create a test pattern, split a zone, export, and open the inspector. SwiftUI
describes menus with a [[Commands]] value, attached to the app's scenes in [[LineScopeApp]]
(Chapter~\ref{ch:model}).

<<LineScope/Commands/LineScopeCommands.swift>>=
import LineScopeCore
import SwiftUI

struct LineScopeCommands: Commands {
    <<Commands: finding the target session>>

    var body: some Commands {
        <<Commands: File menu, test patterns>>

        <<Commands: File menu, export and close>>

        <<Commands: Filter menu>>

        <<Commands: Resample menu>>

        <<Commands: View menu>>
    }
}
@ \section{Which document does a menu act on?}

Menus are global, but their actions are for one document: the one in the frontmost window.
SwiftUI's way to connect the two is a \emph{focused value}. Each document window publishes its
[[Session]] with [[focusedSceneValue]], and [[@FocusedValue]] here reads the value of whichever
window is key.

That alone has one bad consequence. The inspector is a separate window, and while the user works
in it, \emph{it} is the key window. It publishes no session, so the focused value is [[nil]], and
every menu item would turn gray at the moment the inspector is showing the image one wants to
filter. So the menus fall back to [[ActiveSession.shared]], the session of the most recently key
document window, which the document windows keep up to date (Chapter~\ref{ch:views}). When a
document window is key, the two agree. When the inspector is key, the fallback names the document
it describes.

<<Commands: finding the target session>>=
@FocusedValue(\.session) private var focusedSession
@Environment(\.openWindow) private var openWindow

/// Falls back to the last active document so the menus keep working while the inspector is key.
private var session: Session? { focusedSession ?? ActiveSession.shared.session }
@ Every action below starts with [[session?.]]. With no document open, the optional chain makes an
action do nothing, and a [[.disabled(session == nil)]] makes that visible.

\section{The File menu}

Test patterns don't need a document: choosing one opens a new window for it. The value passed
to [[openWindow(value:)]] is the pattern's specification with default size and parameter.
SwiftUI finds the [[WindowGroup]] that presents that type (Chapter~\ref{ch:views}). The
[[Custom…]] item opens the generator window, where size and parameter can be set first.

<<Commands: File menu, test patterns>>=
CommandGroup(after: .newItem) {
    Menu("New Test Pattern") {
        ForEach(TestPatternKind.allCases) { kind in
            Button(kind.displayName) { openWindow(value: TestPatternSpec(kind: kind)) }
        }
        Divider()
        Button("Custom…") { openWindow(id: TestPatternGeneratorView.windowID) }
            .keyboardShortcut("n", modifiers: [.command, .option])
    }
}
@ Export and Close Tab both act on the \emph{focused variant}: the tab last selected or clicked
in any zone. The original can't be closed, because every other variant is derived from it and
every pane falls back to it.

<<Commands: File menu, export and close>>=
CommandGroup(after: .saveItem) {
    Button("Export Image…") { session?.exportFocused() }
        .keyboardShortcut("e", modifiers: [.command, .shift])
        .disabled(session == nil)
    Button("Close Tab") {
        if let session { session.closeVariant(session.focusedVariantID) }
    }
    .keyboardShortcut(.delete, modifiers: [.command])
    .disabled(session?.focusedVariant.isOriginal ?? true)
}
@ \section{Filters and resampling}

A menu item either runs its operation at once or asks for parameters first. Items that need
parameters end in an ellipsis, as the macOS convention asks. They set the session's
[[pendingRequest]], and the document window reacts by presenting the parameter sheet. The menu
never shows user interface itself. The others call [[perform]], which runs the operation in the
background on the focused variant and adds the result as a new tab. Since the source is whichever
tab is focused, operations chain naturally: blur a copy, then shrink the blurred copy.

The filters are grouped as the people using them think of them: smoothing, then sharpening and
edge detection, then point operations on the colors.

<<Commands: Filter menu>>=
CommandMenu("Filter") {
    Group {
        Button("Gaussian Blur…") { session?.pendingRequest = .gaussianBlur }
        Button("Box Blur…") { session?.pendingRequest = .boxBlur }
        Button("Median 3×3") { session?.perform(.filter(.median)) }
        Button("Noise Reduction…") { session?.pendingRequest = .noiseReduction }
        Divider()
        Button("Sharpen (Unsharp Mask)…") { session?.pendingRequest = .unsharpMask }
        Button("Edge Detect (Sobel)") { session?.perform(.filter(.sobel)) }
        Button("Laplacian") { session?.perform(.filter(.laplacian)) }
        Button("Emboss") { session?.perform(.filter(.emboss)) }
        Divider()
        Button("Grayscale") { session?.perform(.filter(.grayscale)) }
        Button("Invert") { session?.perform(.filter(.invert)) }
    }
    .disabled(session == nil)
}
@ The Resample menu is generated from [[ResampleMethod.allCases]]. A method added to that
enumeration therefore appears in the menu without any change here. Every method needs a scale
factor, so every item opens the sheet.

<<Commands: Resample menu>>=
CommandMenu("Resample") {
    ForEach(ResampleMethod.allCases) { method in
        Button("\(method.displayName)…") { session?.pendingRequest = .resample(method) }
    }
    .disabled(session == nil)
}
@ \section{The View menu}

The View menu repeats the split and unsplit buttons of the zone headers, so they have menu
items for keyboard users. It also holds the display option ``Actual Device Pixels''. That option
lives in the session, so the menu adapts it with a [[Binding]] whose getter and setter go through
the optional session. The inspector gets the shortcut Command-Option-I,
which is conventional for inspectors on macOS.

<<Commands: View menu>>=
CommandGroup(before: .toolbar) {
    ForEach(Zone.allCases) { zone in
        Button("Split \(zone.title)") { session?.split(zone) }
            .disabled(session == nil)
    }
    ForEach(Zone.allCases) { zone in
        Button("Unsplit \(zone.title)") { session?.unsplit(zone) }
            .disabled((session?.panes[zone.rawValue].count ?? 1) < 2)
    }
    Divider()
    Toggle("Actual Device Pixels", isOn: Binding(
        get: { session?.useDevicePixels ?? false },
        set: { session?.useDevicePixels = $0 }
    ))
    .disabled(session == nil)
    Button("Reset Line") { session?.line = .initial }
        .disabled(session == nil)
    Divider()
    Button("Show Inspector") { openWindow(id: InspectorView.windowID) }
        .keyboardShortcut("i", modifiers: [.command, .option])
    Divider()
}
@
